WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:11.820
Another button type we mentioned when we talked about types is the FAB, the floating action button. It's the button usually at the bottom right of the screen,

2
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:24.420
and it stands for the most important action on the screen I'm on. It's floating because it usually has a shadow, raised a little above the background, and in mobile apps it usually sits above the navbar.

3
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:36.440
For example, in the chats tab of a messaging app, there's a floating action button for starting a new chat, which may be the most important action on that screen.

4
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:44.860
I start a new chat to talk to someone, or to look someone up. So the action is very clear to me, sitting in the floating action button like this.

5
00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:58.000
The material design guidelines for the floating action button are very simple. One is that its contrast against the background behind it is clear. Sometimes it takes the same color as a solid background,

6
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:08.320
so the floating action button barely shows, its contrast is weak, and it gets lost in the background. With a different color, it stands out from the background.

7
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:22.360
Another key guideline, when it isn't an extended floating action button: its icon must express the action it takes, especially since there's no label next to it saying exactly what it does.

8
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:36.200
In this example, the plus sign, depending on context, is expected to let me add a post, or show some actions above it to choose from. The chat icon is expected to start a new message, or open the messages page,

9
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:50.640
depending on context. But the icons on the other side may not be clear at all, and I can't predict what they do until I try them first.

10
00:01:50.640 --> 00:02:03.880
That's another very important guideline. The extended floating action button is a regular floating action button with a label or text next to it, describing the icon or the action the user takes when tapping it,

11
00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:11.360
like the example in an email app: I tap the compose floating action button to write or send a new email.

12
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:24.660
Often the two are combined: there's an extended floating action button, and when I scroll down it collapses into a regular floating action button, just an icon, and when I reach the end of the screen

13
00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:37.320
it expands back into an extended floating action button, like this example: at the top it's extended, when I scroll it collapses, and at the end of the screen it's extended again.

14
00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:51.520
If I scroll back up it doesn't change, because I'm going back up, not down to new content I'm seeing for the first time. That's also the usual behaviour of the extended and regular floating action button in the material design guidelines.

